ly took another jump  very similar to tesla in some instances  that the company  has been focusing on really promoting as  of itself as this  digital or premium electric car  manufacturer  but also like trying to to make it more  appealing to the  the middle class consumers in china  we are always very clear about our  target user groups  for example the price of our et7 is  similar to the ones of the best-selling  mid-size  or full-size sedans in china like bmw's  five-class  audi a6 mercedes-benz e-class  neo isn't a recent startup that came out  of nowhere  chinese ev startups started to pop up  around 2014  the year neo was founded because of  subsidies offered by the chinese  government  european government china itself they  have all come out and said that they  want to  move into cleaner uh transportation  solutions which essentially at this  point means electric vehicles  china started to promote electric  vehicles back in like  almost a decade ago so at that time it  was only a thousand  uh electric vehicles and still sold in  annually  fast forward to 2018 and annual sales  were easily eclipsing one  million cars in china and william lee  the founder of neo who's also  no stranger to the auto market saw that  spike and decided to make the jump into  the ev market  who himself is somewhat of a serial  entrepreneur  somewhat light like elon musk the  company founded before this was bit auto  and it's a online based service platform  for  for vehicles in general he's kind of a  person with really deep knowledge  in both the it business but also the  automotive industries  but nia wasn't always on a clear path to  success  there's a reason ev stocks are so  volatile  and it's because at any moment companies  are teetering on the edge of bankruptcy  and neo was no exception it was a  smallish  fairly new car company  that faced these really major  fire related recalls early in the year  and then its sales were withering and  for that there are many reasons  its most formidable arrival which is  tesla entered the chinese market in  2019.  alongside competition from tesla sales  of electric cars plateaued in 2019  partly because of decisions taken by the  chinese government  the chinese government cupped the direct  purchase subsidies for electric vehicles  by over 50 percent  in 2019 which is like the largest ever  cut compared to previous years so for  some particular automakers  the the subsidy card really have a huge  negative impact on them  the company started bleeding money it  lost about 1.6 billion dollars in 2019  the valuation kind of shows the picture  here the  the stock really tanked it's been a very  rough  few months for this company they've had  to deal with layoffs they're trying to  reduce this workforce by  about 14 the troubles became so intense  that uh the company came out and said  that it might not have liquidity for  even 12 months but in early 2020  neo's fortunes changed while china's  subsidy cuts almost sank the company  an investment from a chinese province  saved it  neo managed to strike a deal with this  uh  chinese city of cafe in february 2020  they basically did this help neo  injected the cash flows and help them  to deal with the cash flow issues as  part of the deal  neo agreed to establish headquarters in  the city of jefe's economic and  technological development area  as well as provide investors a 24.1  percent stake in the company  it also got um you know credit  facilities from local banks  to the tune of almost one and a half  billion dollars and that kind of really  let the  liquidity concerns take a backstage both  for investors and also the company  to take it mind off the money game and  kind of really focus  on making those cars and delivering  those cars  investors were confident that with  liquidity problems taking a back seat  neil could finally tackle the monumental  task of scaling up the manufacturing of  its vehicles  unlike tech firms that can reach  billions of customers through the  internet  large car makers need to manufacture  products at enormous scale and rely on  huge supply chains now the traditional  auto companies  got this really packed down  but it has also got more than 100 years  of experience behind it  so scaling up was a big problem for  tesla because as  musk famously said tesla was not trying  to invent the machine which means the  car  but actually the machine that makes the  machine which means  tesla or musk was were trying to  re-imagine  the auto manufacturing process and  manufacturing is what neo is hoping will  give them an edge  while their es6 suv costs more than a  third more than that of tesla's model 3  sedan  neo is using innovative features and  services to bring their costs down  the biggest thing that neo is doing  differently from tesla is that it has  outsourced its manufacturing to a  state-owned company which has more  experience in this matter  instead of building a factory of its own  like tesla does with its gigafactories  neo uses a contract manufacturer the  state-owned jac  motors another big difference is neo's  subscription model  that aims to simplify ownership by  taking away the risks of owning the  battery part of the vehicle  and so what the customers would do is  just buy this car or the car shell so to  speak  and and rent the battery from neo  for a monthly fee so if you choose a  subscription model it actually reduced  the price by around like  uh 10 000 rmv which is like 20  or 15 of the upfront price for for a new  car  the final difference between tesla and  neo is how they both approach the  problem of  range anxiety which is the term for how  ev owners worry that their battery will  run out before they reach their  destination  when your battery runs out you have to  park into a charging station and wait  for sometimes for hours until your you  know battery is  charged up enough for you to hit the  road back right  so what neo is uh suggesting people do  is make use of their battery swap  services so in this  a new car user would essentially drive  into this  uh swapping station and in a matter of  few minutes  neo says about three minutes their  depleted battery could be taken  out and replaced with a fully charged  battery and they just go back on  and go back and hit the road while tesla  tried their own battery swap service in  2015  it was discontinued due to lack of  interest neo was banking on the fact  that a battery swap service back in 2015  was ahead of its time  the market was just not mature enough  people were just not as  comfortable with evs at that point neo  says they're working on a second  generation of swapping stations  and plans to add 300 more of those  stations around china  in 2021 the chinese ev market is set to  surge to 1.8 million vehicles in 2021  and neo plans to build a hundred and  fifty thousand  cars quadrupling its output of 43  000 vehicles from 2020. however neo's  competition is about to get much  stiffer they won't just be facing  competition from tesla  and other chinese ev manufacturers  global automakers are also doubling down  on china's ev market recognizing the  gross potential of it  volkswagen and toyota they all  start to increase the investments in  ev technologies along with their chinese  joint venture partners  and whether neo can not only fend off  competition in china  but carve out a path for itself around  the world to become a household name  remains to be seen

